{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_xmyocxh2onoafxo2lenpwx5bmaphns32fpbcojdy7kdjeaxfexaq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. The translation is complete and verified.\n\n## What the library is\n\n`c_src/` is a single 98-line translation unit — a cut-down `cute_c2` 2D collision library. Every function has external linkage, so **all 10** land in the `.so`'s dynamic symbol table. There are no namespace-renaming macros, so linker names equal source names.\n\n## Completion gate — passed\n\n`nm -D --defined-only` on both `.so` files, diffed:\n\n```\nc2AABBtoAABB  c2CircletoAABB  c2CircletoCircle  c2Clampv  c2Dot\nc2Maxv  c2Minv  c2Sub  c2V  collided\n```\n\n**10 C exports, 10 Rust exports, empty diff** — and no stray Rust symbols leaked into the table.\n\n## ABI verified against real codegen\n\nI disassembled the C library rather than guessing. It confirmed the SysV classification my `#[repr(C)]` structs must reproduce: `c2v` (8 B) packed in one xmm; `c2Circle` (12 B) → xmm(n)=`p`, xmm(n+1)=`r`; `c2AABB` (16 B) → xmm(n)=`min`, xmm(n+1)=`max`; second struct arg in xmm2/xmm3.\n\n## Behavioral equivalence\n\nI built a `dlopen`-based harness calling both libraries with identical inputs and comparing **raw bit patterns** — 13.7 M checks per run (specials cross-products, random bit-pattern floats incl. NaN/±0/denormals/inf, out-of-range enum tags, and a dense geometric hit/miss sweep). Result across 5 seeds: **68.6 M checks, 0 mismatches.**\n\nTwo fidelity traps worth flagging:\n\n- **`c2Maxv`/`c2Minv`** use the raw `a > b ? a : b` comparison, *not* `f32::max`/`min`. The C ternary returns the second operand on NaN and always returns the second for `±0.0`; `f32::max` is NaN-suppressing. Using the stdlib helpers would have been a silent behavior change.\n\n- **`c2Dot` NaN payloads** — the one place the C source has **no stable behavior of its own**. gcc at `-O0` emits the y-multiply with `b.y` as destination and sums with the y-product as destination; at `-O2` it reassociates to `a.y` and the x-product. Same source, different output bits. Since x86 `mulss`/`addss` propagate the *destination* operand's NaN, this is observable (e.g. `nan` vs `-nan` under `printf`). I matched the **unoptimized** build, because the shipped `CMakeLists.txt` sets no `CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE` (and derives its project name from the parent directory — it is the harness's own build file), so `cmake && make` compiles with no optimization. A three-way test over 50,625 NaN-rich combinations confirms: **Rust ≠ C(`-O0`) in 0 cases**, and every Rust-vs-`-O2` difference is one where C(`-O0`) itself disagrees with C(`-O2`) — **0 unexplained**. Inputs with at most one distinct NaN payload agree under either ordering, so this only bites in the genuinely compiler-dependent corner. It's documented in the source.\n\nBugs and quirks were preserved, not fixed: the `collided` argument swap in the AABB-vs-circle arm, the `default: return 0` arms, and the bitwise-`|`-then-`!` idiom in `c2AABBtoAABB`.\n\n`cargo build --release` compiles clean from scratch, zero warnings; internals are safe Rust apart from the two required pointer reads in `collided` (via `read_unaligned`, so misaligned callers can't trigger UB). `c_src/` is untouched and my scratch build artifacts are removed — the deliverable is `translation/Cargo.toml` and `translation/src/lib.rs`."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":731,"cache_read_input_tokens":60646,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":731,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"da1a2aec-6f74-4e8a-9ca4-4e4078bb73b9","uuid":"8d22af9e-fc3b-490d-bed1-16f21b5a92ce","timestamp":"2026-08-26T19:45:14.002Z"}
